<<^ code="&#x0002;">>

<<~ ahu #/head>>

! The dyad across the ladder ~ what the field has measured

A tasked spirit went out over the model where ''the human never gets a key'' — a key belongs to a human-device DYAD, which holds a private EDGE, and a public Face is an opt-in declaring act — across four rungs: ''local · household-nexus · community civic-nexus · Region civic-nexus, with Regions interacting''.

⚠ ''THE SEARCH ENGINE WAS DOWN FOR THE WHOLE SWEEP.'' The 200-call budget was spent before the spirit was invoked; six lanes worked through direct fetches on guessed URLs plus the HN, GitHub, OpenAlex, Crossref, PubMed and Wikipedia APIs. ''Reddit was unreachable at the tool level'', removing the richest source of individual //"I lost everything"// narratives. So coverage is strong where a primary URL is guessable and thin where discovery was required, and ''unreached items are marked rather than filled from memory''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-load-bearing-negative>>

!! ★ Nobody has built this. Anywhere. At any scale. ★

> ''Nobody has built or deployed "identity as a signed dyadic edge between a person and a device" as a named, general-purpose primitive.'' Not in SSI, not in object-capability, not in local-first, not in petname theory, not in any shipped system reachable.
>
> The SHAPE has decades of prior art. But ''every instance frames the edge as person↔person or process↔process, never person↔device.'' The device is the substrate the key sits on, ''not a party to the relation''.

Held at high confidence across four independently searched literatures. And the term is ''free'': PubMed returns ''zero'' for `"human-device dyad"` and `"person-device dyad"` — the worry that health-sensing had already taken the phrase does not hold under that index.

!!! The theory is deep and the building is absent

* ''Petname systems'' (Stiegler 2005; the 2024 Spritely revival by Lemmer-Webber, Miller, Larson, Sills, Yaacoby) are the strongest foundation for //identity is an edge//: a petname is //"for a particular person, for a particular application… a one-to-one mapping between a key and a petname"//. ''A name is not a property of the named thing; it is a fact about a relation.'' Spritely adds EDGE NAMES — paths through intermediaries, `Alyssa⇒Ben Bitdiddle`. ''Conceptual; no shipped implementation found.''
* ''Zooko's Triangle'' — the petname answer does not square it, it ''REFUSES'' it: give up global human-meaningful names, keep meaning local to a relation. The oldest formal statement of why the move is necessary.
* ''Cameron's Law 4, "Directed Identity"'' (2005) is the closest law-level analogue — but frames directionality as ''a property an identifier HAS'', never as the identifier BEING a two-party relation object. Close, and short.
* ''Object-capability'' — Miller's core claim is that a capability IS a reference: connectivity, not property. Fuchsia, Capsicum, seL4 ship real capability routing. ⚠ ''In no shipped ocap system are the endpoints of an edge "a person" and "a device."'' They are processes and components. The revocation pattern to inherit — ''caretaker/membrane: revoke the EDGE, not the principal'' — is exactly right and ''entirely untested at human scale''.
* ''Local-first'' (Kleppmann et al. 2019) names the hole verbatim: //"access permissions for documents beyond secret URLs remain an open research question."//

!!! ⚠ And the flagship deployment of the adjacent idea is DEAD — for economic reasons

''Sovrin'' deployed pairwise relational identity at scale. ''The foundation was formally dissolved 2025-05-21''; MainNet now runs ''read-only on a single cloud server with writes permanently disabled''. The cited cause is ''absence of validator economic incentives'' — volunteer stewards stopped paying to run nodes, >$2M debt accrued.

> ''The model was not refuted. Nobody could sustain the infrastructure it required.'' That is a different and more dangerous failure than being wrong.

`did:peer`, the closest formal analogue to a dyadic identifier, remains ''v1.0 Draft'' — 32 stars, 469 commits, 28 open issues. Real engineering, negligible footprint. ⚠ And structurally: ''DID Core is subject-centric by design''; pairwise-ness is bolted on by a METHOD, never baked into the standard.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-house-exception>>

!! ⚠ "Nobody has built this" is true of the FIELD and false of this house

`packages/lararium-mesh/src/dyad.ts` stands — ''written, typed and tested, and called by no live path''. Its only consumer outside itself is `tests/dyad.test.ts`; `index.ts` re-exports it and nothing reaches for it. So the model is ''SPECIFIED IN CODE'' rather than running, which is a stronger state than a design meme and a weaker one than a wired path, and the distinction is the one this corpus keeps having to re-learn (`crossingDirection` and the recovery keel stand the same way).

Its opening line is the model:

> ''dyad — the vessel×veil RELATIONSHIP, made first-class.'' A dyad names neither a place nor a person: it names ''ONE HUMAN'S RELATIONSHIP WITH ONE DEVICE''… the dyad does not JOIN two identities; ''the dyad ENACTS the identity-relationship''.

Under an operator ruling of 2026-07-26 that says it plainly: ''IDENTITY ALWAYS NAMES A RELATIONSHIP, NEVER A THING.''

⚠ ''The sweep's headline negative holds for the field and NOT for this house.'' It is a finding about the world, and a reader who carries it inward will go looking for something to build that already stands.

⚠ ''AND THE DOCTRINE MEMES DO NOT CARRY IT.'' The word `dyad` appears in `persona-policy` and two docs; ''the model lives in a source comment'' at `packages/lararium-mesh/src/dyad.ts`. A search of the memegraph for this model finds prose about it and never the thing itself, so ''the corpus reads as though the model were unbuilt''. That is the gap this section closes and the reason to close it: a memegraph that cannot find its own foundations sends every reader back to the field.

!!! THREE NAMES, THREE JOBS — and this is the answer to the person-node challenge

* the ''BINDING'' — the PersonaGroup's keys, //"which make the fleet a fact rather than a claim"//
* the ''INTERNAL name'' — one usable label over that group, ''private and local, for the human alone''
* the ''HANDLE'' — an optional public pet-name over the same group, ''and the only one that ever federates''

!!! ★ The tiering the membership-CRDT family cannot offer ★

Written in `dyad.ts` before the field raised the objection, and answering it exactly:

> ''A foreign peer verifies presented edges and never sees the fleet; only co-members hold the wider graph. The membership-CRDT family cannot offer that split — it buys convergence by replicating the op graph to everyone, handing each member THE ROSTER AND THE DEVICE-TO-HUMAN LINKAGE.''

Keyhive and DCGKA ''are'' that family. So the challenge — //the person-node is surplus, delete it// — has a priced answer: ''deleting it costs the tiering''. A group of device keys converges by handing every member the map of who is whose.

!!! And the fleet never instantiates

> ''the fleet EVALUATES AS A QUERY over that binding and never instantiates: nothing stores a fleet object to seize, forge, or sync.'' The internal name never crosses a wire, so //"a captured vessel spills the dyads admitted TO IT and never the human's map of themselves."//

`fleetOfGroup(dyads, groupRootDid)` filters by ''the group's signature'', never a label — //"a fleet assembled from labels would let a forged record walk into a human's own map of themselves."// ⚠ ''An UNBOUND dyad joins no fleet'': absence of a binding reads as //not yet gathered//, never as membership in a default group. And a verify that THROWS reads as refusal, //"an unreachable check must never WIDEN a fleet"//.

''The Venn-diagram of fleets needs no model'' because there is no fleet object to diagram: each PersonaGroup queries its own from the same dyads, and overlaps are simply devices holding dyads bound to several groups.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-convergent-shape>>

!! ★ Four unrelated teams reached almost your model — with the person-node DELETED ★

The strongest positive result in the sweep, and the sharpest challenge in it.

* ''① Ink & Switch, Keyhive'' — the closest live prior art, stating the model nearly verbatim: //"An individual is identified by a single Ed25519 public key… a group is a collection of other principals"//, and they model //"a person (or more specifically **their authority**) as a group, with each of the persons devices being an individual member of the group."// ''Key rotation is adding a new individual and removing the old.'' Revocation is group-membership CRDT merge; post-compromise security via BeeKEM. ⚠ ''Note the vocabulary: "their authority", never "them".''
* ''② Apple's iCloud Keychain syncing circle'' — genuinely ''dyad-recognises-dyad, deployed to a billion devices, and never marketed as such''. Each device generates its OWN P-384 keypair, never a copied key. A new device joins by ''sponsor / applicant / voucher'': an existing device signs a voucher attesting the applicant. ''That is "the remaining relations recognise the new dyad", not "prove you know the master secret."''
* ''③ SSB Fusion Identities'' — //"each device still uses its own identity"// while devices //"share fusion identities"//. ''Designed, never shipped.''
* ''④ UCAN v1.0.0'' states the principle at RECOMMENDED level: //"Private keys themselves SHOULD NOT move from one context to another… unique to each physical device and unique per use case… to limit blast radius."// Authority travels by delegation — ''sharing authority without sharing keys''.

!!! ⚠ The argument the field would raise, and it is not a refutation

> Keyhive, DCGKA and Signal-style multi-device all converged on ''"N independent per-device principals reconciled by a group-membership protocol."'' Keyhive is explicit: an individual IS a public key; the person IS the group.
>
> ''This is not a refutation of the model — it is the model with the person-node deleted as unnecessary.'' Whether the person-node earns its place, and what it carries that the group does not, ''is the sharpest open question this sweep raises''.

A second reading, weaker but worth holding: the right relational unit may be ''(person, relationship-context)'' rather than (person, device) — pairwise DIDs, petnames and Cameron's Law 4 all put the edge between two PARTIES, with the device as plumbing where that edge's key happens to live. ''On that reading the dyad picks the wrong second endpoint.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-law>>

!! ★ THE LAW — where a keyless design grows a key ★

> ''Every deployed system that markets "no single key" grew exactly ONE human-held secret — protected by RATE-LIMITING rather than by ENTROPY — and none of them publish how often it survives.''

Ranked by how quietly it happened:

# ''Synced passkeys → the platform account.'' The human's Apple/Google/Microsoft password and its recovery path silently becomes the root of every credential. ⚠ A documented case: ''an account suspension destroyed all stored passkeys irreversibly'' — a vendor's moderation decision as a total identity-loss event.
# ''A sovereign region's root → two foreign vendors.'' The German EUDI wallet requires Google Play Integrity or Apple App Attest. ''The state cannot attest its own citizens' devices.'' 3,042 reactions on the objecting issue.
# ''Signal → the PIN.'' A design that removed the key grew a four-digit one, hardened by ''SGX plus a 5-guess Raft limit rather than by entropy''. Matthew Green names the dark pattern and his own capitulation: //"I chose the simplest PIN that the app would allow me to, which was 9512."//
# ''Matrix → the 48-character recovery key.'' Identity-as-mutually-signing-devices ships with one string whose loss means resetting your identity in public.
# ''Apple → the iCloud Security Code.'' //"upon reaching the 10th failure, the HSM cluster destroys the escrow record and the keychain is lost forever."//
# ''Encryption riding on an AUTHENTICATION credential.'' //"When you overload a credential used for authentication by also using it for encryption, the 'blast radius' for losing that credential becomes immeasurably larger."// A user deletes a passkey unaware it was the encryption root; the photos are gone. ''Credential-manager delete dialogs carry no warning at all.''
# ''The phone number as ambient root.'' SIM-swap remains the most-documented total-loss vector at consumer scale. ''A design can hold no key and still be owned by whoever holds the number.''
# ''The designs that refused a last artifact pay openly.'' SSB and Nostr have no hidden root — and pay in ''routine identity discontinuity''.

> ''THE TRILEMMA: hold a last artifact, hold a vendor, or accept discontinuity. No deployed system has found a fourth option.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/authority-survives-memory-does-not>>

!! ⚠ The claim holds for AUTHORITY and fails for MEMORY

//"Losing a device is losing one edge"// survives for standing. It does ''not'' automatically survive for what the standing was holding:

* ''Keybase'': //"there's no way for adding a new device from a paper key to transfer chat history."//
* ''Matrix'': an identity reset leaves prior messages ''permanently undecryptable'' — and the reset is ''publicly legible to your correspondents''.
* ''Keybase revocations stay visible'' on the sigchain, marked revoked. ''A severed edge remains legible AS a severed edge'' — an append-only property worth taking.

And the mechanism that makes it worse: ''an edge that both authenticates and decrypts turns edge-loss into data-loss''. If a dyad's edge carries both, cutting it destroys the memory.

!!! What the field measured about re-establishing a dyad

* ''Keybase reset'' creates //"a new subchain… a new 'eldest' signing key"// — device list, followers and third-party proofs all severed. ''Re-founding, never recovery.''
* ''Reset friction is deliberate and expensive'': made costly because //"adversaries in the middle can pretend to be me and act like I've reset my account"// — one user reported ''5 days locked out''. ''The delay IS the security property.''
* ''Paper-key retention: NO PUBLISHED NUMBER EXISTS ANYWHERE.'' The most-requested figure in the brief, absent industry-wide.
* ''Matrix "unable to decrypt": 322 issues across three repos, 2016→2026'', 283 in element-web alone. ⚠ ''Count how many of the nine enumerated causes are RELATIONAL rather than key failures'' — the edge exists, the key exists, and the ''recognition'' failed to propagate. ''For a model built on edges, that is the failure taxonomy you inherit.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-attestation-seam>>

!! ⚠ Attestation is the seam where sovereignty leaks

''A dyad is only as sovereign as whatever vouches that the device is a device.'' Play Integrity / App Attest is the field's only deployed answer, and it hands that vouching to ''two foreign corporations with unilateral revocation'' — exactly the property the model exists to eliminate.

The objection raised against the German wallet is precisely a dyad objection: //"If someone lands on a sanctions list, they close your account without further notice"// — ''the attesting party is not the region; it is a foreign vendor that can unilaterally sever the dyad''.

⚠ And a distinction to keep: ''smartcards attest IDENTITY; attestation attests DEVICE INTEGRITY.'' Different problems, and the model needs both.

!!! The in-the-wild attack on exactly this shape

''Storm-2372'' (active Aug 2024, reported 2025-02-13): a Russia-aligned actor impersonates a contact, sends a meeting invite carrying an attacker-generated device code, the victim enters it ''on the legitimate login page'' — and the actor then ''registers rogue devices and mints refresh tokens''.

> ''A rogue device enrolling itself as a legitimate dyad is the deployed, in-the-wild attack on this model's shape.'' The cross-device flow that makes borrowed-screen login possible is the same flow that lets an attacker inject an edge. ⚠ RFC 8628 names the risk ''in the spec itself'', and it was exploited exactly as written.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/a-copied-key-cannot-be-retrofitted>>

!! The W3C's own reviewers, on the model's core claim

Traced through the spec repo: Adam Langley opened `w3c/webauthn#1658` (Aug 2021) proposing `devicePubKey` to //"address some of the challenges of introducing syncable credentials"// — mint a local non-exportable key alongside a synced one so a relying party can detect novelty. A reviewer's live objection:

> //"I can't really see what's the benefit of the new device key. It is authorized on first use by a signature chain from an already-registered, synced key… So if the desire is to only rely on device-bound keys, it seems like that promise is already broken by the time the new device-bound key is registered."//

''A copied key cannot be retrofitted into a device-bound one.'' The W3C's own reviewers said so, in public, in the spec repo. ''Direct field support for the model's founding inversion.''

⚠ ''And the counter-measurement, which cuts against on pure friction.'' Microsoft's published synced-passkey figures across hundreds of millions of accounts: ''99% registration success · 3s vs 69s sign-in · 95% vs 30% success rate'' against legacy auth. Whatever the security argument, ''the copied-key model is winning on measured friction'', and a dyad model has to beat those numbers or say why it need not.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-partition-the-world-refuses>>

!! ⚠ The dyad presumes a partition the world does not honour

And presumes it hardest where the stakes are highest.

* ''Matthews et al., CHI 2016'' (103 citations): //"device and account sharing was common, and **mobile phones were often shared despite being considered 'personal' devices**."// Six sharing types; the drivers are ''trust and convenience'', never access-control design.
* ''Pew, US smartphone-only households by income (2025)'': ''<$30k: 34%'' · $30–70k 19% · $70–100k 10% · ''$100k+: 4%''. ⚠ ''The low bracket is getting WORSE (27%→34% since 2021) while the top trends toward redundancy.'' ''A per-device credential model degrades precisely for the population least able to absorb a lost second edge, and the gap is widening.''
* ''ITU 2024'': mobile ownership age 10+ is 95%+ in high-income countries and ''56% in low-income'' — ''~44% of adults there own no phone at all''. ''The floor beneath "one device" is "zero devices, borrowed access."''
* ''London phone theft'': 91,481 (2019) → 117,211 (2024), ''~321 phones per day''; one 2025 case traced 40,000 snatched phones shipped to China.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-screenless-hole>>

!! The thinnest ground in the whole sweep

> ''No literature at all was found on how a person↔device relation forms when the device has no interface.'' Every deployed answer forms a ''device↔account'' or ''device↔hub'' relation and lets the human's presence be ''implied by physical proximity to a printed code''.

* ''SSH host keys and TOFU'' remain the oldest deployed answer, and the documented failure is ''warning fatigue'' — administrators habituate past the mismatch prompt. ⚠ ''TOFU makes the FIRST dyad-formation moment unverified by construction.''
* The deployed pattern family — BLE provisioning, SoftAP, QR-on-a-sticker, NFC tap, claim-code on the box — is ''the dyad formed by a SECOND device acting as the screen'', with a physical secret bridging them.
* ''Matter commissioning'' (setup code + Device Attestation Certificate) is the current standard; failure rates and the resale/transfer //"who owns this device now"// semantics were not reached. ''FIDO Device Onboard'' is the actual standard for this problem; adoption unverified.

''If the dyad is the unit, the screenless device is where the model has the least prior art to stand on.''

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-ui-bound-adversary>>

!! ★ The household's authorized principal IS its adversary ★

''Freed et al., "A Stalker's Paradise", CHI 2018 (Best Paper), 89 participants.'' The spirit calls this the sharpest articulation of the household problem anywhere in its sweep:

> Intimate-partner attacks are typically ''technologically unsophisticated'', carried out by a ''"UI-bound adversary"'' — an ''authenticated, adversarial user operating through the normal interface'' — which //"undermine[s] the predominant threat models under which systems have been designed."//
>
> ''At household scale the authorized principal and the adversary are frequently the same authenticated principal. No amount of credential strength addresses this.''

⚠ ''Read straight onto the dyad model:'' a dyad is an edge between a person and a device, and a UI-bound adversary ''holds a lawful edge''. Every cryptographic property the model offers is intact while the attack succeeds, because the attack never touches the crypto. ''The household rung is not a key problem.''

!!! Coercion is a STATE MACHINE, not a state

''Matthews et al., IEEE S&P 2017'' frames survivor technology use as a three-phase lifecycle — ''physical control → escape → life apart'' — each phase needing different support. So identity-under-coercion carries ''a specific escape transition'', and //nothing in the corpus treats "leaving the household" as solved//.

''Bellini et al., CHI 2023 (Best Paper), 158 survivors + 16 financial advocates'' — four abuser motivations, exploiting shared financial accounts.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-incidental-user>>

!! A new identity category the model has no name for

''"I would have to evaluate their objections" (2021), a 386-person representative US survey'' names the ''INCIDENTAL USER'': //someone who did not choose the device but is captured by it//.

⚠ ''A dyad presumes the human CHOSE the device.'' An incidental user has an edge nobody deliberately formed — and on this model an edge is the whole of identity, so the category is not a UX edge-case, it is an ''unmodelled principal''.

!!! And any single sharing default is empirically wrong somewhere

* ''"We, three brothers have always known everything of each other" (2021), 59 participants across Bangladesh · Turkey · USA'' — sharing behaviour varies ''by culture'', so one global default (share ⊥ isolate) is wrong for a large fraction of the world.
* ''"Everyone Has Some Personal Stuff" (2019), Bangladesh, 21 participants, a 3-week deployment'' — built a ''dual-account shared-password / secret-password'' model because ''single-device-multi-user is the NORM there'', never the exception.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-community-rung>>

!! Community networks read robust and measure fragile

''"Detecting and Mitigating Points of Failure in Community Networks", IEEE Trans. Computational Social Systems, 2019'' — graph analysis of ''ninux.org'', Italy's largest community network. Despite the community's stated goal of a robust self-organized network, the analysis found it //"fragile under several aspects."//

> The //"avoid unnecessary management structures"// philosophy shared by ninux, Guifi.net, Freifunk and NYC Mesh ''reads as robust until formally analysed''.

''The only directly measured technical-fragility result at community scale in the sweep'', and it argues the rung's self-organization can be structurally brittle ''in ways its participants cannot see from inside''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/cross-region>>

!! ⚠ Cross-region is a WARNING, not a postmortem

> ''Cross-region is the most argued and least empirically realized rung.'' Estonia and Aadhaar are pure //postmortem// — real incidents, real numbers, real remediation clocks. Cross-region is currently ''high-quality prediction and expert consensus of concern, not yet high-quality postmortem''. If cross-region is first-class in your model, ''you are not building against measured failure — you are building against a warning''.

The consensus is large and precisely counted: the eIDAS open letter carries ''552 scientists and researchers from 42 countries'' as of 2023-11-26, plus NGOs — Preneel, Cremers, Murdoch, Hoepman, Schwabe among them. ''A separate, later 38-signatory industry letter'' (Mozilla/EFF) is a different document; the spirit had conflated the two and corrected itself.

Two mechanics it names as load-bearing:

* ''Article 45a(4)'' — a member state's designated keys are revocable ''only with that government's permission''.
* ''Article 45(2a)'' bans browsers from imposing security checks beyond what is expressly permitted — ''an UPPER BOUND on security rather than a floor'', inverting the normal direction of security regulation. The historical anchor is ''DigiNotar (2011)'', whose CA compromise was used to surveil Iranian users' traffic — the exact failure the article would make harder to prevent.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-reversal>>

!! The spirit reversed itself on Aadhaar ~ and the reversal is the useful part

It had reported Aadhaar as an unambiguous exclusion story. ''The field disagrees with itself'', and both sides are large:

* ''Muralidharan, Niehaus & Sukhtankar (2020)'' — stricter biometric ID ''reduced corruption'' and ''1.5–2 million legitimate beneficiaries lost access at some point during the reform''. The authors attribute the adverse effects ''primarily to how the TRANSITION was managed'', not to biometrics as such.
* ''Muralidharan, Niehaus & Sukhtankar, AER 2016'' — randomized rollout, ''157 subdistricts, 19 million people'': payments faster, more predictable, less corrupt, ''without adversely affecting access'', and beneficiaries overwhelmingly preferred it.

> The disagreement tracks ''implementation quality and transition management'', not the underlying concept. ''The measured harm at region scale sits in the MIGRATION, not the architecture'' — which is precisely where //"re-establishing a dyad"// lives.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/what-stayed-dark>>

!! What the sweep could not reach

!!! Verified ABSENCES — findings, not search failures

* ''No published figure anywhere for Keybase paper-key retention.''
* ''No published Matrix device-verification completion rate'', and ''zero academic papers on Matrix cross-signing UX'' in OpenAlex or Crossref — for the largest live deployment of this model's core mechanism.
* ''No vendor publishes end-to-end recovery-success telemetry'' for any system in the report.
* ''No measured operational-friction data for X-Road'' — the one clean no-common-root two-region federation, silent across two independent passes.
* ''PubMed: zero results'' for both dyad term-forms.
* ''No literature on person↔device relation formation for screenless devices.''
* ''Nobody has deployed identity-as-a-person-device-dyad at community scale — or at any scale.''

!!! Unresolved rather than absent

Confirmed ''unresolved rather than absent'': Khera / Drèze primary sources and Jharkhand ration-denial numbers · Swedish BankID elderly-fraud statistics and MitID 2021–22 migration complaints (likely Swedish/Danish-language, outside the indexes reached) · ''EUDI Large-Scale Pilot interim findings — project sites 404 / DNS-failed'' · Guifi.net's Compact text · the Pan-Canadian Trust Framework · ICAO PKD failure data · Sardex quantitative outcomes · Decidim's actual census-verification mechanism.

⚠ ''X-Road's operational friction is undocumented in open sources.'' The most-cited cross-region system in the world publishes no measured account of where it rubs.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/edges>>

